<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ma-i</h1><p class="st-meta">971 CE – 1339 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Philippine trading polity known from Chinese records of the 10th to 14th centuries, among the earliest named states of the archipelago.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> history records merchants from Ma-i bringing goods to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> in 971 and again in 982. Its location is debated: most scholars favour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindoro">Mindoro</a>, others Bay on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laguna-de-bay">Laguna de Bay</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Zhao Rugua's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-fan-zhi">Zhufan zhi</a> of 1225 describes a settlement of over a thousand families on a river, whose chief used a white umbrella. Chinese traders anchored offshore and handed goods to local merchants, who carried them to other islands and returned to pay in beeswax, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, pearls and tortoiseshell. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> Buddhist images were said to stand in the countryside.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ma-i appears in Chinese texts until the 14th century and then drops from the record. Later Chinese and Spanish sources do not mention it, and its trade passed to ports such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tondo">Tondo</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-maynila">Maynila</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ma-i.jpg" alt="Ma-i" width="320" height="444"><figcaption>Image: Boxer Codex · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naturales_4.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>971 CE – 1339 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous Philippine religion</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/philippines">Philippines</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mait, Ma-yi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1155&amp;focus=ma-i" aria-label="Ma-i on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8061 1753 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ma-i lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8381 2009h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8381 2009h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Philippines. <a href="/atlas?year=1155&amp;focus=ma-i">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

